1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,160
Think about this: Which date should
automatically be used as the claim

2
00:00:04,160 --> 00:00:06,340
deadline? Let’s work through it together.

3
00:00:06,920 --> 00:00:11,500
I'm Doctor Gainz. Lesson 6: Build a
jurisdiction checklist.

4
00:00:12,120 --> 00:00:16,380
A useful checklist records who holds the
funds, how the relevant dates are

5
00:00:16,380 --> 00:00:20,660
measured, who may submit a claim, what
evidence is required, and where

6
00:00:20,660 --> 00:00:24,980
disputes are decided. Add whether a
business entity needs counsel and

7
00:00:24,980 --> 00:00:28,520
whether a proposed assignment or fee
arrangement has special requirements.

8
00:00:28,520 --> 00:00:32,900
A checklist with unknown answers is more
useful than a confident guess.

9
00:00:33,760 --> 00:00:37,720
For each requirement, retain a short note
and an official source link.

10
00:00:38,100 --> 00:00:42,300
Label the requirement as confirmed for the
specific sale type, awaiting

11
00:00:42,300 --> 00:00:44,820
clarification, or outside your role.

12
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:49,920
Do not copy an old deadline into a new
case without verifying what event

13
00:00:49,920 --> 00:00:54,540
starts the clock. Source publication
dates, sale dates, and claim deadlines

14
00:00:54,540 --> 00:00:58,660
are different fields. Keep the checklist
current without rewriting the

15
00:00:58,660 --> 00:01:02,880
underlying evidence. When guidance
changes, preserve the former version date

16
00:01:02,880 --> 00:01:05,140
and explain what needs professional
review.

17
00:01:05,660 --> 00:01:09,880
Your checklist organizes research; it does
not decide a legal question.

18
00:01:10,580 --> 00:01:11,560
Pause and try this.

19
00:01:12,300 --> 00:01:14,580
Build a blank checklist with ten
requirements.

20
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:19,040
Fill only facts that a current official
source actually establishes.

21
00:01:19,420 --> 00:01:20,860
Leave the rest awaiting review.

22
00:01:20,860 --> 00:01:25,860
Quick knowledge check. Which date should
automatically be used as the claim

23
00:01:25,860 --> 00:01:30,900
deadline? The answer: Neither; verify the
applicable event and rule.

24
00:01:31,480 --> 00:01:34,580
Different claim procedures use different
triggering events and periods.

25
00:01:35,120 --> 00:01:38,040
Keep the source, the date, and the
unknowns visible.

26
00:01:38,540 --> 00:01:39,640
I'll see you in the next lesson.
